WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.255
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.255 --> 00:00:03.630
DAVID THORBURN:
I swore to myself

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:06.915
that if I became a lecturer, I
would never bore my students,

00:00:06.915 --> 00:00:11.910
that that was a worse sin than
almost any other a teacher

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:13.245
could commit was to be boring.

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:19.530
SARAH HANSEN: Today on the
podcast, The Film Experience.

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:21.330
In this episode,
we'll explore what

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:24.270
it takes to reframe
historical artifacts, things

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:28.283
like classic plays, books, and
films, for a modern audience.

00:00:28.283 --> 00:00:29.700
DAVID THORBURN:
And I said to him,

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.990
what did you actually think
Shakespeare's environment was?

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:37.740
He somehow had managed to
disconnect the literary power

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.140
of Shakespeare from
the fact that it

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.970
was done on this
incredibly popular stage.

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:44.760
And it was so sensational.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.240
And it was so much more like
television or the movies

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.130
than it was like some process
of sitting in a library,

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:56.430
in wood-paneled splendor,
reading words in silence.

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:57.900
That's not what Shakespeare was.

00:01:00.967 --> 00:01:02.550
SARAH HANSEN: How
can we compare media

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.920
so that students today
might better understand

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.860
what it originally was?

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.960
Welcome to Chalk
Radio, a podcast about

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.940
inspired teaching at MIT.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:15.660
I'm your host, Sarah Hansen,
from MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.462
My guest today is a
longtime professor at MIT.

00:01:18.462 --> 00:01:19.920
DAVID THORBURN:
I'm David Thorburn.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.490
I'm a professor of literature
and comparative media.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:29.335
I've taught The Film
Experience for 39 years at MIT.

00:01:29.335 --> 00:01:31.710
SARAH HANSEN: We'll be talking
about Professor Thorburn's

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:35.610
class, 21L.011, The
Film Experience.

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:39.000
And like all of the classes
we talk about on this podcast,

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.000
you can check out The Film
Experience on OCW's website

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:43.000
at ocw.mit.edu.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:50.410
Professor Thorburn made a
splash in the academic world

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.360
by bringing in media
that traditionally

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.130
hadn't existed there.

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:56.980
We'll start with how this all
came about and what it was like

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:00.490
for him to challenge what
might be considered unholy

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:02.835
in the sacred space
of the classroom.

00:02:02.835 --> 00:02:04.210
DAVID THORBURN:
The story of what

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:08.470
made me into a film scholar, or
a media scholar is, at least,

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:12.080
interesting to me and
still surprises me.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:14.740
I had been teaching at Yale
for seven or eight years,

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:18.070
and I was offered a chance to do
a visitation at the University

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:20.740
of California in Santa Barbara.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.660
And I was trying to
introduce the students

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.980
to the idea of conventions.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:27.830
What it means when you begin
a story once upon a time,

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:29.180
and every story begins that way.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:31.180
Or what it means when you
say they lived happily

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:33.790
ever after to take the
simplest example of what

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:35.350
a convention is.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:38.020
I was trying to illustrate
how, in fact, we internalize

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.060
these things, and we
understand the conventions

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:42.670
of the narratives of our
own time in our blood

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:44.797
without even thinking about it.

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:46.630
So what I needed to
illustrate the principle

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:48.880
were shared texts.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:51.850
So I began by asking them--
this was in the 1970s,

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:54.010
in the early 1970s--

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:56.560
and I asked the students there--
these are bright California

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.440
public school students--

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.470
how many of you have ever
seen a John Wayne movie?

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:05.560
Maybe 15% of the students
raised their hand.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.830
Then I said, well, how many of
you have read Huckleberry Finn?

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:10.630
Maybe 30%.

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:13.480
I tried other novels,
classic novels.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:15.370
I tried Shakespeare plays.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:18.780
I was at a loss-- remember,
this is in the early '70s.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:24.220
Finally, I said, how many of
you have seen All in the Family?

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:26.350
For those in the audience
too young to know this,

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:30.220
All in the Family was a
television program, a classic

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:33.520
and transforming one,
that appeared in 1971

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:36.580
on American television and
involved a working class

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:37.720
hero who had never--

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:40.150
such characters had never
appeared on television before.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:42.580
It was a very important program.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:45.970
And of course, 100% of
the class had seen it.

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:49.660
And I began to take my examples
from television programs.

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:51.830
And I began-- this wore on me.

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:54.940
It wore in on me as
the term went on.

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:58.370
And what I realized was that
the literature of our own day,

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:00.730
the story forms that
belonged to my students

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:03.280
and to the generation
of kids I was teaching,

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:05.470
were visual, not literary.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:14.720
So I became interested
in television

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:16.220
as a narrative medium.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:17.990
And then I came to
realize, as I began

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:20.660
to think more seriously
about the role of television

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:24.140
in American life-- remember, by
1970, American television was

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:27.020
the dominant form of
entertainment and narrative

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:28.280
in American life.

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:31.250
And, in fact, this
initial understanding, out

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:34.460
of which my whole interest in
media developed, has shaped--

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:37.520
I realize now just in
talking about this--

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:39.650
my way of teaching
the film experience.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:46.880
When I got back to Yale
after my year's visitation,

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:49.370
I introduced a course called
Literature and Popular

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:53.450
Culture, in which I explored
these ideas in greater depth.

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:55.010
I read some bestsellers.

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:57.710
I ended the course by
looking at The Honeymooners.

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:00.830
And I remember, I was
teaching the course in a room

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:02.930
at Yale College called
Linsly-Chittenden

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:07.490
Hall, in which legendary figures
had lectured on Shakespeare.

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:09.380
And one of these
legendary professors,

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:12.770
the man who had actually hired
me at Yale, Maynard Mack--

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:15.110
a great literary scholar--

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:18.230
poked his head in while I was
teaching The Honeymooners,

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:20.660
and then later called
me into his office

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:23.690
and expressed great
disappointment over the fact

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:27.510
that I was polluting
this environment.

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:29.965
So then I-- and we then
had a serious conversation.

00:05:29.965 --> 00:05:31.340
And I said to him,
what did you--

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:35.810
what did he actually think
Shakespeare's environment was?

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:38.855
And that was when it struck
me how narrow my education had

00:05:38.855 --> 00:05:41.480
been because he really had not--
even though he was a great man

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:42.350
and a great--

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:43.850
he knew the history.

00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:47.750
But he somehow had managed to
disconnect the literary power

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:50.120
of Shakespeare from
the fact that it

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:52.980
was done on this
incredibly popular stage.

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:54.740
And it was so sensational.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:58.940
And it was so much more like
television or the movies

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:02.870
than it was like some process
of sitting in a library,

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:07.100
in wood-paneled splendor,
reading words in silence.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.620
That's not what Shakespeare was.

00:06:12.582 --> 00:06:14.040
SARAH HANSEN: Part
of why we wanted

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:16.620
to talk about Professor
Thorburn's film experience

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:20.730
class is because teaching
film changes as technology

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:23.100
and student life evolve.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:25.770
I asked him what this evolution
means for the ways in which he

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:27.630
presents his materials.

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:28.950
His answer?

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:31.858
Start from the very beginning.

00:06:31.858 --> 00:06:33.900
DAVID THORBURN: When I
begin the film experience,

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:35.730
I ask the students
to do something

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:40.230
they find increasingly
difficult. In the old days,

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:44.400
before the proliferation
of smartphones

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:47.250
and visual media all over
the place and everyone

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:50.160
being able to make his
own video and so forth,

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:53.400
and the ubiquity of DVDs--

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:56.460
before those days, I was able
to tell the students, look,

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:58.500
we live in an audio-visual age.

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:00.300
We live in a televisual age.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:01.890
I want you to think
that stuff away.

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:03.180
I want you to get--

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:05.460
think yourself back to
an age before people

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:08.640
understood that there is
such a thing as a film.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:12.060
And think of what the
original meaning of movie is.

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:13.920
Go back to the root meaning.

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:16.380
It's a metaphor that is
a dead metaphor for us.

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:18.270
But it actually--
it tells you what

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:20.340
the most fundamental
aspect of the movies

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:24.090
were for the original audiences
and why they were so fascinated

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:27.990
by the capacity of this new
technology to capture motion.

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:29.940
It seemed like-- and it was--

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:32.250
a tremendous new
advance in technologies

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:35.790
of the representation of
human experience because it

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:37.460
captured humans in motion.

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:39.840
It captured the
movements of the world.

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:42.660
The first films, as I
try to show my students,

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:46.770
were so preoccupied by this
wondrous capacity of the medium

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:48.750
that it's almost the
only thing they filmed.

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:52.860
And you can see the evolution
of the medium embodied

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:58.170
in the way in which, after an
initial profound fascination

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:01.830
with the novelty of
movement begins to wear off,

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:05.350
other properties of the film
medium begin to be discovered.

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:06.960
So I tell my students
this principle.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:09.480
And I ask them to try
to think their way back

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.970
into the attitude of
these first viewers, who

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:13.590
didn't know about
the visual media,

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:16.350
to play a kind of
thought experiment.

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:18.180
Today, it's much
harder to make them

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:20.580
play that experiment
because I have to tell them,

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:24.150
as I do in that first lecture,
think away your iPads.

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:25.890
Imagine a world without iPads.

00:08:25.890 --> 00:08:28.020
Imagine a world
without smartphones.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:31.905
Imagine a world without
instant communication.

00:08:31.905 --> 00:08:34.700
And of course, this is much
harder for the students to do.

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:36.590
And of course, at
one level, they

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:40.520
are much more
capable of processing

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:43.080
audio-visual
information than I am,

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:48.095
and than my parents' generation,
and than all of the generations

00:08:48.095 --> 00:08:49.970
older than they are,
because they've grown up

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:52.010
in an environment in
which, essentially, they're

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:56.930
surrounded 24/7 by
audio-visual stimuli,

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:59.690
by audio-visual signals.

00:08:59.690 --> 00:09:01.490
Many people might see
this as dangerous.

00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:02.480
Perhaps it is.

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:04.330
But it develops new capacities.

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:07.880
And one can see this in
the history of television

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:09.980
in which, at a
certain point, there

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:11.660
are certain kinds of
television programs

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:13.910
that become too complex
for older generations.

00:09:13.910 --> 00:09:15.410
And only the younger
generation that

00:09:15.410 --> 00:09:17.035
was used to going to
the movies and had

00:09:17.035 --> 00:09:19.700
been watching television
and had been watching MTV--

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:24.243
they were able to
understand this very easily.

00:09:24.243 --> 00:09:25.910
SARAH HANSEN: Professor
Thorburn's class

00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:28.400
includes a two-hour
lecture, with one more

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:30.050
hour of discussion.

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:32.750
In our conversation, he
talks about the changing

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:35.990
popular opinion surrounding
lectures, namely,

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:38.990
that they're outdated and less
effective than other styles

00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:40.190
of teaching.

00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:42.530
In short, he disagrees.

00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:44.030
I'll let him explain.

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:46.280
DAVID THORBURN: I've thought
a lot about what it means

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:48.200
to lecture to undergraduates.

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:49.970
I hated bad lectures.

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:53.640
I thought they were the
pits of higher education.

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:56.420
And I swore to myself that
if I became a lecturer,

00:09:56.420 --> 00:10:00.860
I would never bore my students,
that that was a worse sin

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:05.510
than almost any other a teacher
could commit was to be boring.

00:10:05.510 --> 00:10:10.400
And I have also felt that
in recent years especially--

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:14.030
although it's always been an
undercurrent in education--

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:17.000
there's been a kind of
growing negative feeling

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:19.850
about lectures as if
they're herding students

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:21.380
into a herd experience.

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:23.520
And it doesn't give them
hands-on experience.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:27.620
And I think, intellectually,
that's a foolish perspective.

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:29.240
Professors know
more than students.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:32.630
It's appropriate for students to
listen to professors sometime.

00:10:32.630 --> 00:10:34.940
Professors should take
the responsibility

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:37.480
when they talk to
students very seriously.

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:40.790
And in a small classroom, they
should certainly encourage

00:10:40.790 --> 00:10:42.560
discussion and interaction.

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:43.890
And I think at my best--

00:10:43.890 --> 00:10:45.620
I'm not sure I
always am at my best.

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:46.820
I know I'm not.

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:48.350
But when I am at
my best, I think

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:53.990
I'm doing something that can't
be captured in printed form

00:10:53.990 --> 00:10:56.210
or couldn't be captured
if you gave students

00:10:56.210 --> 00:10:58.790
a transcript of
what you had done,

00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:00.805
which is that I'm
modeling thinking.

00:11:00.805 --> 00:11:04.280
I'm modeling how you
make connections.

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:13.910
It seemed to me, as I thought
about the most inspiring

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:16.760
lectures that I had
as an undergraduate,

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:18.560
there was one professor
I had especially.

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:22.320
He's now dead, but he was a
wonderfully inspiring man.

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:24.260
His name was Lawrence Holland.

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:26.630
His lecture was on
American literature.

00:11:26.630 --> 00:11:29.660
And his strategy in
lectures I've tried to copy.

00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:32.570
One thing he always did was
he presented an outline.

00:11:32.570 --> 00:11:36.210
It was not the text
of the lecture,

00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:41.150
but it was three or four
central, digestible points

00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:43.640
that organized the
lecture in sequence.

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:46.290
It didn't capture
the whole lecture,

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:49.790
but you could use that sheet
to organize your understanding

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:52.010
of the lecture.

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:54.170
I thought that was a
wonderfully helpful--

00:11:54.170 --> 00:11:57.463
I found it wonderfully
helpful both for review

00:11:57.463 --> 00:11:58.880
but also because
what it showed me

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:02.090
was that professor Holland
had planned what he was doing,

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:05.330
that he was presenting material
to me that he had thought long

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:07.820
about, that he had
organized in a serious way.

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:12.200
But the second great thing
about Larry Holland's lectures

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:15.612
was that they were not
completely written out.

00:12:15.612 --> 00:12:17.570
There were parts of them
that were written out,

00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:20.380
very eloquent moments often
at the beginning and the end.

00:12:20.380 --> 00:12:22.250
And I've sometimes
imitated that.

00:12:22.250 --> 00:12:24.080
But the greatest
part of his lectures

00:12:24.080 --> 00:12:26.660
was his lectures
seemed spontaneous.

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:28.850
Even though he was
following his lecture,

00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:31.340
it seemed as if he was not.

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:33.230
He was speaking to me.

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:34.790
And I tried to capture--

00:12:34.790 --> 00:12:36.770
I've never been
as good as he was.

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:40.530
There was a kind of passion, a
kind of feeling in what he did.

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:43.850
And I realized that
another aspect of what

00:12:43.850 --> 00:12:45.650
he was showing me was how--

00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:48.650
not just intellectually
engaged, but how passionately

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:52.910
engaged, how emotionally
valuable studying literature

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:53.430
could be.

00:12:55.952 --> 00:12:58.410
SARAH HANSEN: Maybe the most
telling feature of this course

00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:00.810
is its intended
outcome for students.

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:03.900
Professor Thorburn isn't trying
to create film critics, or even

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:05.640
experts, in his classes.

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:07.888
His goal is much
more significant.

00:13:07.888 --> 00:13:10.430
DAVID THORBURN: One of the ways
that the study of literature,

00:13:10.430 --> 00:13:13.690
the study of film differs from
the study of technical things,

00:13:13.690 --> 00:13:17.450
and the reason I teach it, is
that it belongs to everyone,

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:19.440
that it's valuable for everyone.

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:22.060
Not everyone needs to know
about quantum mechanics.

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:23.825
But I believe everyone should--

00:13:23.825 --> 00:13:25.200
and if they don't,
I feel they're

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:28.350
impoverished-- know how
to read a good story,

00:13:28.350 --> 00:13:31.620
enjoy plays, know how
to enjoy the movies.

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:33.690
So I feel that what
I'm giving my students

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:36.272
is in some sense something
even more valuable to them

00:13:36.272 --> 00:13:37.980
because it will be
something that they'll

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:40.560
have with them for their whole
lives, their whole careers.

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:44.850
One of the things MIT students
sometimes often do not realize

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:46.410
is that a very
large number of them

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:49.890
do not end up making
their living in the areas

00:13:49.890 --> 00:13:51.390
in which they majored.

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:53.670
But all of them end up
wanting to go to the movies.

00:13:53.670 --> 00:13:55.530
All of them end up
with the capacity

00:13:55.530 --> 00:13:57.690
to read and enjoy literature.

00:13:57.690 --> 00:13:59.700
And I hope that coming
out of my classes,

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:01.680
they'll do those
things with greater joy

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:05.220
and with greater intelligence.

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:07.080
One thing I've
thought about that--

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:10.510
I don't-- I haven't actually
said this in my courses yet.

00:14:10.510 --> 00:14:15.180
But I think implicit
in my film course

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:18.410
is an admiration for a
certain kind of achievement,

00:14:18.410 --> 00:14:20.730
an admiration for a
certain kind of artist,

00:14:20.730 --> 00:14:23.940
an artist like Jean Renoir,
the great filmmaker,

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:28.920
or Monet, the great painter,
whose cathedral series I often

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:31.170
talk about in my teaching.

00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:33.510
And I realized that I
shouldn't leave it implicit

00:14:33.510 --> 00:14:38.030
because I want to set up as a
candidate for their admiration

00:14:38.030 --> 00:14:43.020
an alternative to Wall
Street, an alternative

00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:45.780
to entrepreneurial genius.

00:14:45.780 --> 00:14:49.950
I admire Jean Renoir's genius
or Orson Welles's genius

00:14:49.950 --> 00:14:52.770
or James Joyce's
genius universes

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:55.650
more than I admire
what entrepreneurs do.

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:59.690
I respect what
entrepreneurs do, but I

00:14:59.690 --> 00:15:02.240
am in awe of what
great artists do

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:05.690
and of what great doctors do,
while we're on the subject.

00:15:05.690 --> 00:15:07.760
We've become so
preoccupied by what

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:11.000
we might call commercial or
financial success instead

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:14.970
of the kind of lasting
success that great art

00:15:14.970 --> 00:15:19.130
or the practice of medicine
or the practice of nursing

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:23.660
or, dare I say it, the practice
of teaching might also embody.

00:15:23.660 --> 00:15:32.632
[LAUGHS]

00:15:32.632 --> 00:15:35.090
SARAH HANSEN: Professor Thorburn
shares additional thoughts

00:15:35.090 --> 00:15:37.670
on teaching The Film Experience
in a series of videos

00:15:37.670 --> 00:15:41.420
within the Instructor Insights
section of his OCW course.

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:45.830
You can find them
at ocw.mit.edu.

00:15:45.830 --> 00:15:48.260
And while you're there,
download the teaching resources

00:15:48.260 --> 00:15:51.410
from this course and find
so many other materials

00:15:51.410 --> 00:15:54.125
shared by faculty across
all of the schools at MIT.

00:15:57.380 --> 00:15:59.090
Thank you for listening.

00:15:59.090 --> 00:16:01.190
We're so glad that
you're tuning into what

00:16:01.190 --> 00:16:05.120
makes MIT such an exciting
place to learn, conversations

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:07.250
with people passionate
about impacting

00:16:07.250 --> 00:16:09.230
the world in positive ways.

00:16:09.230 --> 00:16:11.960
We hope you'll open
MIT to more people

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:15.530
by sharing this podcast and
OCW with your own colleagues

00:16:15.530 --> 00:16:16.670
and friends.

00:16:16.670 --> 00:16:18.830
Until next time,
I'm Sarah Hansen,

00:16:18.830 --> 00:16:21.170
from MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:16:21.170 --> 00:16:24.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

